 should think about these systems  especially the large language models as  being cultural technologies what do i  mean by cultural technology i mean a  technology that allows humans to access  and summarize and use all the other  knowledge um and discoveries that other  humans have made over the over the  generations  uh so it's not a technology that itself  is intelligent but it's one that's sort  of essential for human intelligence  because it allows humans to access the  intelligence of other humans and you  could make a good argument that the sort  of er example of this is language itself  which enables us to say  tell another hunter that we found game  or it lets a grandmother and  grandmothers are probably very much the  relevant category here pass on  information uh about uh foraging to the  next generation um  uh writing is so language is sort of the  er example of this kind of cultural  technology  but i think there's a good argument that  much of human progress has been the  result of changes and progress in these  cultural technologies so writing itself  is another example of something that now  enables you not only to  access the knowledge of the people close  to you but the knowledge of people who  are hundreds of years past or or  hundreds of miles away um and print  obviously turns up the dial on writing  so once you have print you can access  even more information from even more  people and there's good reason to  believe that print was responsible for  the enlightenment for liberal democracy  for a lot of the changes for  industrialization for a lot of the  changes in the in the long 18th century  um  uh libraries are another example of kind  of cultural technology that enables us  to access more information and of course  more recently we have the very  phenomenon of internet search itself and  phenomenal like wikipedia that are also  these technologies that don't actually  produce knowledge or information  themselves but give us more efficient  ways of accessing the knowledge or  information that other people  before us or in different places have  actually produced  and it's just wrong to think about these  cultural technologies as like agents so  you know asking whether lambda is  intelligent or not is like asking  whether the university of california  berkeley library is intelligent or not  or whether google search knows the  answer to your questions or not it's  just the wrong category um and what i  want to argue is that lambda and other  ais are this kind of a thing this kind  of a technology not what you might think  of as a classic artificial intelligence  in say robotics where you're actually  trying to design uh an independent agent  that will go out into the world and be  intelligent  now that is not at all to minimize the  importance of these technologies um in  fact there's a pretty strong argument of  p made by people like dick boyd and  peter richardson and joseph henrik that  that cultural ability the ability to  have cultural technologies is exactly  what makes humans special so other  animals do pass on information from  generation to generation to some extent  but none of them does it in the way that  we do and to the extent that we do and  certainly for as long over many  generations as we do and there's a good  argument that it's exactly that ability  to take on information from previous  generations that enables us to uh to  function as well as we do you could  paraphrase newton and say all of us are  standing on the shoulders of all of  those who have come before us um so  adding a cultural technology actually  makes a big difference to uh to human  intelligence um  and of course what large language models  do and other ai models like imagenet or  or or dali do is not produce language  themselves what they do is summarize  billions and billions and billions of  sentences that have been placed out on  the web by other humans  uh  all right so what's missing if if in  fact what's most i