rn about the world  through exploration and trying to find  cause and effect relationships  and what we did was just design a very  simple set of tasks with an online  blicket detector the blicket detector is  the way that we found out about  causation and the way this works is you  just put things on the detector and the  detector lights up sometimes and not  others and you have to figure out how it  works and the uh criterion for how it  works is that at the end we ask the kids  and we do this with three and four and  five-year-olds um  tell me which one is a blicket the  blickets make it go and see if you can  make it work yourself  so it's it's quite a simple task but  children turn out to be extremely good  at it even very young children are very  good at  making inferences about the structure  and we thought this is such a simple  task that surely in those billions of  texts that uh that something like gpt3  is using there should be the information  that you need to know what the right  consequence of what the right answer to  these questions are um and there is some  work and some evidence that in some  contexts um something like jpt can can  do  everyday reasoning but again there's a  lot of everyday reasoning already  embedded in the text that it's learning  from and in fact no one had ever seen  this particular machine with this  particular structure with this  particular pattern before in all of  those texts and all of that time until  the children are actually seeing it  so what we discovered was even in this  online setting which is kind of  interesting in and of itself the  children are very good at exploring  they'll try different kinds of  combinations and try and test them do  kind of everyday experiments and in  about 20 trials they solve the problem  we also tested sort of state-of-the-art  reinforcement learning mechanisms on  this and they take about 150 000 trials  to solve this problem and uh here's an  example of a child actually playing with  it so you can see how uh you can see how  it works  um  and you know in terms of innovation as i  said i think it's interesting 20 years  ago when we started doing this work the  kids would not have been happy about  doing this on a computer screen and now  they're perfectly happy about doing it  on a computer screen um  but when we gave it not only to gpt 3  but also to palm  which is the even more recent and uh and  more powerful version  they were surprisingly bad at it and it  was only if we gave them specific  training about this is a blicket and  this isn't that they did well at all  um  okay so that suggests that it gpt 3  isn't doing everything that you would  want to do to have intelligence  intelligence depends both on being able  to access information from the past and  being able to innovate and do things in  the future now as i was saying the the  prevailing view about the evolution of  human intelligence is that this kind of  cultural technology is really crucial to  that intelligence that raises the  question okay is having is uh uh  is turning up the capacity for cultural  technologies a good thing or a bad thing  and  going back you know it's good in the  sense that that's what we evolved to do  but going all the way back in human  history there's always been both sides  so the fact given humans racist sexist  and irrational and biased and have all  these problems in their cognition  depending on the cognition and knowledge  and beliefs of other people is always  going to be this double-edged sword  because on the one hand you're going to  get the information about where the game  is but on the other hand and  evolutionary biologists have argued this  um there's now an incentive for the  other hunter to mislead you about where  the game is so he can get all the game  uh himself  and  with each iteration of new cultural  technologies this tension between  getting the truth from other people and  being deceived by other people has has  been uh uh ha