lution  um  and the way that people have dealt with  this over the years is that as each of  these cultural technologies emerges  there's a new set of norms rules  regulations laws this is something that  i know you've been talking about in this  group  that emerged to deal with this  double-edged sword of cultural evolution  um and we've shown an empirical evidence  that even  very young children even four-year-olds  uh have norms about being truthful so  for example if you have someone lie to a  kid several times they'll stop trusting  that person so they're already using  information about the past uh about the  past testimony and reliability of past  testimony to make new inferences and  there's a whole  giant bunch of evidence that suggests  that that's true  um so this is even with three and  four-year-olds and then we have  institutions like testifying under oath  we have  uh new categories of people  like editors or journalism professors or  journalism schools  fact checkers we have new laws libel  laws uh privacy laws defamation suits  and so forth  it wouldn't make any sense to have had a  fact checker before you actually had uh  before you had print it wouldn't make  any sense to have a fact checker in the  context of uh say an oral society  um so i think the punchline which i  imagine is kind of a punchline here as  well  is that it's we shouldn't be thinking  about these things in terms of is this  you know the golem who's going to come  and kill us all in our beds because it  will become an ascension agent i think  you might want to worry about that but  one of the things i say is as a mother  one of the things you learn how to do is  ration your worries to the ones that are  going to be worse  but you certainly could worry about the  potential effects of a new form of  cultural technology a new form of  cultural evolution and do all the kinds  of things that you uh that we've done in  the past to make sure that that cultural  technology uh is ends up 